From steve@webdevelop.com Wed Nov  5 12:36:27 1997
Message-ID: <199711051647.LAA07650@michael.webdevelop.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Nov 97 11:46:10 -0500
From: Steven Kokinos <steve@webdevelop.com>
Subject: Re: NFS

>1- In terms of security, how secure or insecure is the NFS ?  Given that I'm
>an ISP and I have fears that anyone who has an NFS client can access the
>mounted HD.  This is what I was told!  How true is it?  How idiot am I ;-)

When you export a file system via NFS you can choose what machines can 
have access to that file system.  If the machine isn't listed then 
nothing other than your NT box will be able to mount the unix fs.  

>2- Is the NFS the best way for me to have the Unix HD mounted by the NT
>server.  Better yet, What alternatives do I have to meet the task?

This is probably the simplest way to do it.  There is also a product 
called Zannet (http://www.zannet.com I think) that allows you to map a 
unix volume to a logical drive on your unix system, similar to the way 
you mount a netware volume.  This might work better for you, but since 
your log files are presumably large, you'll need to do testing as to 
speed.  For instance, NFS reads are generally fast, but writes are slow, 
so depending on what you are doing you could run into bottlenecks.

-Steve Kokinos

