> 
> But longer compilation-time per se is a drawback. I guess we can agree on
> that.
> 
> > BTW: what "current CISC-CPUs" are you thinking of?
> 
> 68060 or PentiumII

threre are more diffs between risc and cisc cpus. *one* difference is
microcode and hard-wired instructions. there're a *lot* more.
for example the PPC 620 specification says it has *more* instructions than
the x86 instruction set has. does this fact will make the PPC620 a cisc
cpu ? 
no.
on the other hand we have the fact that intel can hardwire more and more
of them x86 instr. every year. so if i get you right you would call
intels x86 computers risc if they have (maybe 1998) all instructions hard
wired ?
i would not do so. i would call them a ward-wired cisc or a tuned cisc.

thats the reason why intel will jump out of the x86 one-way-street to them
'merced' risc chip.

risc and cisc cpus are different in there concept and microcode and stuff
is just the outer expression of a basically different design.

