From tsbogend@alpha.franken.de Wed Nov 19 13:45:23 1997
Message-ID: <199711192149.VAA39159@whale.st.usm.edu>
Date: Wed, 19 Nov 1997 23:33:17 +0100
From: Thomas Bogendoerfer <tsbogend@alpha.franken.de>
Subject: Re: cdrecord on Linux/Alpha works
In-Reply-To: <Pine.GSO.3.96.971118102415.23264A-100000@yacht.ee.fit.edu>

> Sorry for my ignorance, but we got an Alpha UDB as an entry-level system
> to learn some of the peculiarities of porting to a 64 it system.  We
> haven't finished our code yet (doh!) to start going 64-bit clean; what is
> "HZ" and its relationship to 100 (or not, in the Linux/Alpha case)?

some timers and timeouts are measured in ticks. One tick is 10ms on 
Linux/Intel, but 1ms (1/1024s to be exact) on Linux/Alpha. The HZ
define gives you the number of ticks per second, so nobody has to
hardcode it in its source, which leads to wrong timings when compiled
on the "wrong" platform.

> I guess my question is will this work on an Alpha?  Will a function
> pointer fit within a void pointer?  Does anyone know about the UltraSPARC? 

sure. And I don't see a reason, why this shouln'd work on UltraSPARC too.

Thomas.

